 of the Gambia River in The Gambia, close to Albreda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jugend-magazine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jugend-magazine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jugend (magazine)</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Jugend was an illustrated weekly magazine of art and literature founded in Munich in 1896 by the publisher Georg Hirth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jugol"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jugol.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jugol</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Jugol is the defensive wall of the city of Harar in eastern Ethiopia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jugurtha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jugurtha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jugurtha was king of Numidia, a grandson of Masinissa who seized the whole kingdom after killing one adoptive brother and fighting the other.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jugurthine-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jugurthine War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 112–105 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Jugurthine War was fought from 112 to 105 BCE between the Roman Republic and Jugurtha, king of Numidia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juho-kusti-paasikivi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Juho Kusti Paasikivi (1870–1956) was a Finnish statesman who served as prime minister in 1944–1946 and as president from 1946 to 1956.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/juka-sambala"><span class="enc-name">Juka Sambala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Juka Sambala was king of Khasso on the upper Senegal River in the mid-19th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/jukun-people"><span class="enc-name">Jukun people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Jukun are a people of the middle Benue valley in north-eastern Nigeria, living mainly in Taraba and neighbouring states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julamerk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julamerk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julamerk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Julamerk, in Kurdish Colemêrg, is the historic name of the town of Hakkâri in the mountains of south-eastern Turkey, above the Great Zab valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-julanda-ibn-masud"><span class="enc-name">Al-Julanda ibn Mas'ud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Julanda ibn Mas'ud was the first Ibadi imam of Oman, elected around 750 by the Ibadi scholars and leaders after the fall of the Umayyad Caliphate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-brunet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jules-brunet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jules Brunet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jules Brunet was a French army officer sent with a military mission to train the shogunate's troops in 1867.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry"><span class="enc-name">Jules Ferry</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jules Ferry was a French republican statesman who twice served as prime minister, in 1880–1881 and 1883–1885.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-gros"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jules-gros.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jules Gros</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jules Gros was a French journalist and geographer whom adventurers in Paris named president for life of the Republic of Counani in 1886.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jules-mazarin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jules Mazarin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jules Mazarin was an Italian-born cardinal and diplomat who served as chief minister of France from 1642 until his death in 1661.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/julfa-nakhchivan"><span class="enc-name">Julfa (Nakhchivan)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Julfa, known to Armenians as Jugha, is a town on the Aras river in Nakhchivan, now part of Azerbaijan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/julfar"><span class="enc-name">Julfar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Julfar was a medieval port on the northern coast of the Oman peninsula, near modern Ras al-Khaimah in the United Arab Emirates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julia-domna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julia-domna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julia Domna</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julia Domna was a Roman empress, daughter of the high priest of the sun god at Emesa in Syria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julia-ward-howe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julia-ward-howe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julia Ward Howe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julia Ward Howe was an American author, poet and social activist who wrote the Battle Hymn of the Republic as new words to the song John Brown's Body.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julian-emperor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julian-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julian (emperor)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julian was a Roman emperor of the fourth century CE, known for attempting to restore traditional paganism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julian-calendar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julian calendar</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Julian calendar is a solar calendar introduced by Julius Caesar in 46 BCE, with a leap year every four years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julian-grenier"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julian-grenier.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julian Grenier</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julian Grenier was lord of Sidon and Beaufort in the crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/julian-revolution"><span class="enc-name">Julian Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · 1925</span><span class="enc-snip">The Julian Revolution was a coup carried out on 9 July 1925 by young officers of the Ecuadorian army who overthrew the government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juliana-of-cornillon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Juliana of Cornillon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Juliana of Cornillon was an Augustinian nun near Liège whose visions led her to campaign for a feast honouring the Eucharist.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-stolberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juliana-of-stolberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Juliana of Stolberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Juliana of Stolberg was a German countess, daughter of Count Botho III of Stolberg, who married William, Count of Nassau-Dillenburg, in 1531.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-the-netherlands"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/juliana-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Juliana of the Netherlands</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Juliana was Queen of the Netherlands from 1948 to 1980, succeeding her mother Wilhelmina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/julien-fedon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/julien-fedon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Julien Fédon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julien Fédon was a free coloured planter of French descent on Grenada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/julien-raimond"><span class="enc-name">Julien Raimond</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Julien Raimond was an indigo plante